enhanceOrbitalInfrastructureArticle("liberty-yard", {
  lead:`<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-ly3b4 writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T12:20:00-06:00 -->Liberty Yard is the working name for an assistant-developed high-orbit assembly and propellant installation serving Project Pilgrim. Its Columbia tug connection, mid-1980s development, XO-100 assembly in 1987–88, and shakedown through 1989 are incorporated infrastructure reconstruction rather than direct authorial canon.${americanOrbitalInfrastructureExtrapolation}<!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->`,
  canon:`<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-ly3c5 writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T12:20:00-06:00 -->Direct canon establishes Project Pilgrim's orbit-built and orbit-resident transition, the 1990 federal break, and the survival of useful program capacity in the corporate successor.${americanOrbitalInfrastructureCanon} The Liberty Yard name, location, assembly, depot and checkout functions, tug dependence, Columbia relationship, chronology, XO-100 work, staffing, operating procedures, later ownership, and every physical specification remain incorporated reconstruction or open.${americanOrbitalInfrastructureExtrapolation}<!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->`,
  sections:[
    {id:"need",title:"Need for a high-orbit yard",html:`<p>Columbia's accessible orbit supported frequent aircraft traffic but was inefficient as the final departure point for a large interplanetary vehicle. Dense docking and construction schedules also made long-duration assembly and hazardous propellant work difficult.</p><p>Project Pilgrim therefore separated the traffic port from the departure yard.</p>`},
    {id:"development",title:"Development in the mid-1980s",html:`<p>Liberty Yard developed with Pilgrim structures, propulsion, life support, and logistics during the middle 1980s. Tugs, tankers, power systems, docking equipment, and storage had to function before full Mars-ship assembly could begin.</p><p>The archive does not fix one commissioning ceremony. The installation acquired capability in stages.</p>`},
    {id:"concept",title:"A yard rather than a city",html:`<p>The installation concentrated docking, truss, storage, propellant, power, control, instrumentation, and assembly functions. Habitation supported construction, inspection, and servicing rather than an independent civic population.</p><p>Public comparisons with the Raumhafen overstated Liberty Yard's resident community and general-port capacity.</p>`},
    {id:"orbit",title:"Orbital position",html:`<p>A higher orbit reduced part of the energy penalty for outward departure and separated the yard from Columbia's ordinary traffic. The exact altitude, inclination, and transfer geometry remain open.</p><p>The location complicated emergency return, resupply, and crew rotation. Every transfer consumed tug time and propellant.</p>`},
    {id:"tugs",title:"Connection to Columbia",html:`<p>Spaceplanes delivered crews, instruments, replacement parts, and many components to Columbia. Tugs then moved selected structures, tanks, stores, and personnel to Liberty Yard.</p><p>Transfer packages required compatible attachment points, mass records, electrical and thermal safing, and destination acceptance.${americanOrbitalInfrastructureExtrapolation}</p>`},
    {id:"layout",title:"Functional areas",html:`<p>Working functions included receiving and inspection, structural assembly, propellant storage and transfer, power and thermal service, instrumentation checkout, limited habitation, stores, and safe-haven capacity.${americanOrbitalInfrastructureExtrapolation}</p><p>These functions do not establish named modules or a fixed external shape.</p>`},
    {id:"assembly",title:"Interplanetary assembly",html:`<p>Large structures, propulsion systems, radiation protection, habitats, return equipment, Mars-flight hardware, and stores could be joined and tested without occupying Columbia docking capacity.</p><p>Assembly records tracked configuration, mass, connections, unresolved work, and responsibility for each installed system.${americanOrbitalInfrastructureExtrapolation}</p>`},
    {id:"fuel",title:"Propellant depot",html:`<p>Storage and transfer were as important as structural work. Tanks arriving from lower orbit were inspected, connected, inventoried, and assigned to tugs, tests, or the interplanetary vehicle.</p><p>Cryogenic boil-off, leakage, contamination, thermal control, and isolation constrained the yard. Exact propellants and capacities remain open except where individual programs establish them.</p>`},
    {id:"pilgrim",title:"XO-100 Pilgrim",html:`<p>XO-100 became the yard's first fixed full interplanetary assembly during 1987–88. Shakedown through 1988–89 exercised propulsion, power, communications, life support, docking, and integrated vehicle control.</p><p>The 1990 crisis prevented the planned federal Mars flight. The assembled inheritance did not disappear.</p>`},
    {id:"staffing",title:"Staffing and work cycle",html:`<p>Construction crews, inspectors, tug crews, engineers, instrument specialists, safety personnel, and visiting program staff rotated through the yard. Continuous permanent population is not established.</p><p>Limited accommodation favored planned work periods and return to Columbia over indefinite residence.${americanOrbitalInfrastructureExtrapolation}</p>`},
    {id:"safety",title:"Safety and rescue",html:`<p>Distance from Columbia reduced collision exposure but lengthened emergency response. Pressure refuges, isolation, independent communications, reserved power, medical stabilization, and a return vehicle or tug plan were necessary for inhabited work.${americanOrbitalInfrastructureExtrapolation}</p><p>Propellant accidents and incomplete vehicles required wider exclusion zones than ordinary station cargo.</p>`},
    {id:"commercial",title:"Commercial inheritance",html:`<p>Pilgrim personnel, hardware, Mars-entry research, and operating experience passed into the commercial successor after 1990. Later corporate Mars vehicles used the Columbia region and inherited yard practice.</p><p>The archive does not settle whether Liberty Yard remained wholly federal, entered a mixed operating arrangement, or was supplemented by separate commercial construction sites.</p>`},
    {id:"status",title:"Status at the canon horizon",html:`<p>Liberty Yard remained a named part of the American interplanetary infrastructure record. Its exact configuration, ownership, traffic, resident complement, and later construction program as of 5 January 2012 are open.</p>`}
  ],
  sectionOrder:["need","development","concept","orbit","tugs","layout","assembly","fuel","pilgrim","staffing","safety","commercial","status"],
  related:[
    {href:"american-orbital-infrastructure-network.html",kicker:"Infrastructure system",label:"American Orbital Infrastructure Network"},
    {href:"orbital-tugs-tankers.html",kicker:"Transfer fleet",label:"Orbital Tugs and Tankers"},
    {href:"deep-space-century.html",kicker:"Vehicle generation",label:"Deep-Space Century"},
    {href:"american-space-propulsion-industry.html",kicker:"Propulsion sector",label:"American Space-Propulsion Industry"}
  ],
  facts:[
    ["Working development",`Mid-1980s ${americanOrbitalInfrastructureExtrapolation}`],
    ["Working first full assembly",`XO-100 Pilgrim · 1987–88 ${americanOrbitalInfrastructureExtrapolation}`],
    ["Working shakedown",`1988–89 ${americanOrbitalInfrastructureExtrapolation}`],
    ["Working access",`Orbital tug from Columbia ${americanOrbitalInfrastructureExtrapolation}`],
    ["Working ordinary character",`Yard and depot, not independent city ${americanOrbitalInfrastructureExtrapolation}`],
    ["Article horizon","5 January 2012"]
  ],
  categories:["American space infrastructure","Orbital shipyards","Project Pilgrim"]
});

enhanceOrbitalInfrastructureArticle("kennedy-lunar-station", {
  lead:`<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-ke4b5 writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T12:20:00-06:00 -->Kennedy Lunar Station is the author-established first continuously occupied human outpost beyond Earth, reaching permanence in late 1979 or 1980 before Germany's Mondhafen Süd.${americanOrbitalInfrastructureCanon} Frequent transport, modular improvisation, emergency-return planning, and the detailed supply system are compatible explanations rather than a directly fixed construction record.${americanOrbitalInfrastructureExtrapolation}<!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->`,
  canon:`<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-ke4c6 writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T12:20:00-06:00 -->The Kennedy name, late-1979-or-1980 continuous-occupation milestone, American priority, and continued growth are directly established.${americanOrbitalInfrastructureCanon} The John F. Kennedy dedication rationale, exact site and opening, first crew, modular architecture, population, reactor, northern drilling camp, scientific and industrial register, Project Pilgrim laboratory, commercial and family settlement details, first lunar birth, government, boundaries, and operating procedures remain incorporated reconstruction or open.${americanOrbitalInfrastructureExtrapolation}<!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->`,
  sections:[
    {id:"race",title:"Race for permanence",html:`<p>Germany possessed stronger heavy transport and robotic preparation. The United States pursued a narrower operational condition: one resident crew had to arrive before the preceding crew left.</p><p>Frequent resupply and modular improvisation substituted for the initial mass and standardization of the later German base.</p>`},
    {id:"name",title:"Name and political dedication",html:`<p>President Buchanan named the outpost for John F. Kennedy, whose 1961 commitment had given the American program a coherent national mission. The dedication claimed an earlier Democratic-Republican achievement as national inheritance.</p><p>The name also made continued appropriation and abandonment politically visible.</p>`},
    {id:"site",title:"Site and approaches",html:`<p>The exact station site is open. American robotic work during 1977–79 emphasized farther northern terrain, while Germany concentrated on the lunar south. Kennedy Station itself is not fixed at the north pole.</p><p>Landing approaches, terrain clearance, communications, power, drilling prospects, dust, and access to field sites shaped local operations.</p>`},
    {id:"first",title:"Continuous occupation",html:`<p>Continuous occupation began in late 1979 or during 1980. The operational criterion was overlap between crews, not a ceremonial date.</p><p>The station became the first human settlement beyond Earth never deliberately abandoned. Opening-day wording and the identity of the first permanent crew remain unresolved.</p>`},
    {id:"architecture",title:"Early architecture",html:`<p>Early quarters, laboratories, stores, power, communications, and emergency shelters came from different program lines. Cargo containers served as storage, exposed services were progressively covered, and regolith shielding accumulated around inhabited modules.</p><p>Later construction did not erase the station's heterogeneous core.</p>`},
    {id:"supply",title:"Supply chain",html:`<p>Canaveral and Columbia formed the Earth-orbit end of lunar logistics. Crew and high-value cargo arrived through the reusable fleet; tankers, tugs, and lunar-transfer systems moved people, propellant, machinery, and stores onward.</p><p>Loads were divided among survival-critical supplies, maintenance spares, scientific equipment, construction material, and items that could wait for a later movement.${americanOrbitalInfrastructureExtrapolation}</p>`},
    {id:"power",title:"Power and life support",html:`<p>A compact fission reactor supplied dependable power as the settlement grew. Life-support work covered atmosphere, water, waste, temperature, food storage, and contamination control.</p><p>Local oxygen and water experiments reduced dependence on delivered consumables gradually. Exact recovery fractions and plant capacities remain open.</p>`},
    {id:"growth",title:"Growth, 1981–1985",html:`<p>The station grew from a small initial crew toward a working estimate of roughly ten to fourteen permanent personnel during 1981–84. Regolith-covered quarters, laboratories, a machine shop, pressurized rovers, oxygen experiments, and a northern drilling camp expanded its functions.</p><p>Population varied with construction and visiting crews. Exact annual figures are not established.</p>`},
    {id:"surface",title:"Surface transport and field work",html:`<p>Pressurized and unpressurized rovers connected landing areas, drilling sites, instrument stations, excavation, and survey routes. Dust control, navigation, communications, battery or power margins, rescue range, and suit maintenance governed every excursion.${americanOrbitalInfrastructureExtrapolation}</p><p>The northern drilling camp belonged to the wider Kennedy system even if it lay beyond the principal habitation cluster.</p>`},
    {id:"work",title:"Scientific and industrial work",html:`<p>Crews studied geology, radiation, reduced-gravity medicine, excavation, landing surfaces, regolith shielding, drilling, repair, and experimental oxygen or water recovery. Machine tools and workshops extended equipment life.</p><p>Early industry reduced resupply and prepared later missions. It did not make the settlement self-sufficient.</p>`},
    {id:"emergency",title:"Emergency and return planning",html:`<p>Pressure loss, fire, reactor trouble, medical crisis, loss of communications, vehicle failure, and surface-stranding scenarios required shelters, isolation, stored consumables, rescue capability, and an available return plan.${americanOrbitalInfrastructureExtrapolation}</p><p>American permanence depended on overlapping transport capacity as well as habitation. A base unable to evacuate or receive repair remained politically and medically fragile.</p>`},
    {id:"pilgrim",title:"Project Pilgrim laboratory",html:`<p>Kennedy became the human-systems laboratory for Mars. Long isolation, closed life support, nuclear power, maintenance, drilling, reduced-gravity medicine, rover operations, and field repair could be tested where rescue remained closer than Mars.</p><p>Results passed into XO-100 and its commercial successor even after the federal expedition was prevented in 1990.</p>`},
    {id:"commercial",title:"Commercial and family settlement",html:`<p>Later companies operated transport, resource, communications, accommodation, and service facilities in the wider American lunar system. Kennedy remained the historical public center while private sites and annexes expanded beyond it.</p><p>Household accommodation and corporate settlement made the system the site of the first lunar birth around the turn of the millennium. Exact date and jurisdiction remain open.</p>`},
    {id:"comparison",title:"Comparison with other lunar settlements",html:`<p>Mondhafen Süd opened with greater standardized mass and industrial purpose. Kaguya emphasized machine-prepared Japanese settlement. Serenity supported a Commonwealth route. Sancta Maria Lunae combined Latin science and religious presence.</p><p>Kennedy's distinguishing strength was its early permanence and connection to frequent American traffic.</p>`},
    {id:"status",title:"Status at the canon horizon",html:`<p>By 5 January 2012 Kennedy belonged to a wider American lunar settlement and industrial network rather than standing as one isolated base. Its exact boundaries, population, civil government, corporate property divisions, and physical module register remain open.</p>`}
  ],
  sectionOrder:["race","name","site","first","architecture","supply","power","growth","surface","work","emergency","pilgrim","commercial","first-lunar-birth","comparison","status"],
  related:[
    {href:"american-orbital-infrastructure-network.html",kicker:"Infrastructure system",label:"American Orbital Infrastructure Network"},
    {href:"orbital-port-columbia.html",kicker:"Orbital gateway",label:"Orbital Port Columbia"},
    {href:"american-lunar-resource-industry.html",kicker:"Later economic system",label:"American Lunar-Resource Industry"},
    {href:"society-kennedy-lunar-station.html",kicker:"Community history",label:"Society of Kennedy Lunar Station"}
  ],
  facts:[
    ["Exact location","Open; station itself not fixed at north pole"],
    ["Working population, 1981–84",`Approximately 10–14 ${americanOrbitalInfrastructureExtrapolation}`],
    ["Working supply gateway",`Orbital Port Columbia ${americanOrbitalInfrastructureExtrapolation}`],
    ["Working field installation",`Northern drilling camp ${americanOrbitalInfrastructureExtrapolation}`],
    ["Working later character",`Public center within wider commercial settlement system ${americanOrbitalInfrastructureExtrapolation}`],
    ["Article horizon","5 January 2012"]
  ],
  categories:["American space infrastructure","Lunar settlements","Permanent human settlements"]
});

enhanceOrbitalInfrastructureArticle("frontier-annex", {
  lead:`<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-fa5b6 writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T12:20:00-06:00 -->Frontier Annex is the working name for an assistant-developed privately financed commercial complex attached to Orbital Port Columbia in 1994–96. Its laboratories, payload services, accommodation, manufacturing, storage, carrier support, and federal-port interfaces are incorporated reconstruction.${americanOrbitalInfrastructureExtrapolation}<!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->`,
  canon:`<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-fa5c7 writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T12:20:00-06:00 -->Direct canon establishes the later private carrier, corporate use of inherited federal infrastructure, Columbia's continuing role, and public authority behind the American corporate-space system.${americanOrbitalInfrastructureCanon} The Frontier Annex name, ownership, 1994–96 chronology, attachment, property and jurisdictional form, functions, federal emergency powers, zoning, utilities, tenants, construction, financing, population, docking plan, and later status remain incorporated reconstruction or open.${americanOrbitalInfrastructureExtrapolation}<!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->`,
  sections:[
    {id:"origins",title:"Origins",html:`<p>Frontier's cargo, satellite, repair, and professional-passenger traffic outgrew leased desks and storage in federal modules. The 1993 National Orbital Carrier charter provided contracts, obligations, and political durability sufficient to finance a dedicated commercial estate.</p><p>The company chose attachment to Columbia because the port already supplied traffic control, rescue, life support, tracking, communications, and regular transport.</p>`},
    {id:"construction",title:"Construction, 1994–1996",html:`<p>The Annex was assembled and connected to Columbia's civil side during 1994–96. Private capital and tenants financed commercial facilities, while design and installation had to satisfy federal docking, pressure, power, thermal, traffic, and emergency standards.</p><p>The exact sequence, module registry, and contractors remain unsettled.</p>`},
    {id:"layout",title:"Functional arrangement",html:`<p>Contemporary descriptions identify laboratories, payload preparation, private communications, short-term accommodation, a small manufacturing bay, commercial-satellite storage, freight staging, and carrier-support areas.</p><p>These functions do not establish a final module-by-module plan. Tenants and equipment changed as the complex matured.</p>`},
    {id:"interfaces",title:"Connection to Columbia",html:`<p>Pressure, power, data, communications, thermal control, docking, and emergency access connected the Annex to the federal port. Some services could be privately metered or locally backed up without becoming fully independent.${americanOrbitalInfrastructureExtrapolation}</p><p>Isolation valves and electrical boundaries allowed Columbia command to separate a damaged or contaminated section.</p>`},
    {id:"traffic",title:"Carrier traffic",html:`<p>Courier supported urgent satellite and return work, Caravan supplied bulk freight, and Wayfarer carried crews and corporate specialists. Older Liberty aircraft and other Frontier services remained part of the traffic mix.</p><p>The Annex gave those vehicles a common receiving, work, and accommodation center rather than treating each flight as an isolated contract.</p>`},
    {id:"tenants",title:"Tenants and work",html:`<p>Tenants included payload owners, satellite firms, laboratories, communications services, manufacturers, insurers, maintenance contractors, and organizations purchasing short-duration orbital work. Exact companies and leases remain open.</p><p>Acceptance required technical compatibility, security review, financing, and an emergency plan.${americanOrbitalInfrastructureExtrapolation}</p>`},
    {id:"property",title:"Private property in a federal port",html:`<p>Frontier and its tenants owned or leased equipment and interior commercial space. They did not own an orbit, approach corridor, shared atmosphere, or right to disregard the station commander.</p><p>The arrangement resembled a privately operated terminal and estate within a publicly controlled port.</p>`},
    {id:"jurisdiction",title:"Jurisdiction",html:`<p>Contracts, employment, intellectual property, tenant rules, and ordinary commercial disputes followed American federal and applicable corporate law. Station safety, traffic, criminal emergency, strategic security, and life-support command remained federal.</p><p>The line produced recurring arbitration because one technical order could impose substantial commercial loss.${americanOrbitalInfrastructureExtrapolation}</p>`},
    {id:"authority",title:"Emergency authority",html:`<p>Fire, collision threat, pressure loss, contamination, rescue, life-support failure, or evacuation placed the Annex under Columbia's technical emergency authority. Federal personnel could isolate modules, redirect vehicles, requisition supplies, and suspend operations.</p><p>Ownership and compensation were addressed after the immediate danger.</p>`},
    {id:"life-support",title:"Shared utilities and accounting",html:`<p>Atmosphere, water, waste, electrical power, cooling, communications, docking time, storage, and crew support crossed the federal-commercial boundary. Metering and service agreements made these resources commercial accounts as well as survival systems.${americanOrbitalInfrastructureExtrapolation}</p><p>Reserve capacity could not be sold down to a level that compromised station emergency margins.</p>`},
    {id:"mars",title:"Mars-program support",html:`<p>Frontier's wider system used Columbia for reconnaissance, cargo preparation, vehicle integration, crew movement, and commercial Mars work after the federal Pilgrim flight was prevented. The Annex housed part of the corporate and technical network behind those missions.</p><p>Exact Mars facilities and consortium tenants inside the Annex remain open.</p>`},
    {id:"competition",title:"Competition and access",html:`<p>The Annex strengthened Frontier's functional position without formally excluding other carriers. Rivals could use Columbia, but Frontier controlled a concentrated estate of services, accommodation, communications, and customer relationships at the principal port.</p><p>Some competitors therefore depended on Frontier facilities even when they did not depend on Frontier vehicles.</p>`},
    {id:"legacy",title:"Legacy and status",html:`<p>The Annex made commercial orbital activity structurally important without producing a sovereign corporate station. It became the early model for private estates embedded in national off-world infrastructure.</p><p>Its precise expansion, ownership divisions, tenant list, and population as of 5 January 2012 remain open.</p>`}
  ],
  sectionOrder:["origins","construction","layout","interfaces","traffic","tenants","property","jurisdiction","authority","life-support","mars","competition","legacy"],
  related:[
    {href:"american-orbital-infrastructure-network.html",kicker:"Infrastructure system",label:"American Orbital Infrastructure Network"},
    {href:"orbital-port-columbia.html",kicker:"Host installation",label:"Orbital Port Columbia"},
    {href:"american-orbital-carrier-industry.html",kicker:"Commercial sector",label:"American Orbital-Carrier Industry"},
    {href:"frontier-functional-monopoly.html",kicker:"Later market position",label:"Frontier's Functional Orbital Monopoly"}
  ],
  facts:[
    ["Working construction",`1994–1996 ${americanOrbitalInfrastructureExtrapolation}`],
    ["Working legal condition",`Private property within a federal port ${americanOrbitalInfrastructureExtrapolation}`],
    ["Working shared systems",`Traffic · life support · rescue · communications ${americanOrbitalInfrastructureExtrapolation}`],
    ["Working federal authority",`Safety, emergency, security, and traffic ${americanOrbitalInfrastructureExtrapolation}`],
    ["Working sovereign status",`None ${americanOrbitalInfrastructureExtrapolation}`],
    ["Article horizon","5 January 2012"]
  ],
  categories:["American space infrastructure","Commercial orbital infrastructure","Orbital Port Columbia"]
});
